🧠 1️⃣ The 5-Second Elimination Pattern

When reading options, eliminate immediately:

❌ Anything with root user
❌ Hardcoded credentials
❌ 0.0.0.0/0 (when security is mentioned)
❌ “Manual process” (when scalability or resilience is required)
❌ “Increase instance size” (when scaling is asked)

This alone removes 1–2 wrong answers instantly.

🔐 2️⃣ Security Pattern Mapping (Most Tested Domain)

If question says:

Keyword in Question	Your Brain Should Say
“Most secure”	IAM Role + least privilege
“Block public access”	S3 Block Public Access
“Encrypt with control”	KMS CMK
“Avoid long-term credentials”	Assume Role / Cognito
“Restrict specific IP”	NACL (explicit deny)
“Private access to S3”	VPC Gateway Endpoint
“Admin access without SSH”	Systems Manager

If you react automatically like this → 80% of security questions become easy.

🏗 3️⃣ High Availability Auto-Trigger Rules

When you see:

Phrase	Correct Direction
“Automatic failover”	Multi-AZ
“Survive AZ failure”	Multi-AZ
“Survive Region failure”	Multi-Region
“Near zero RTO”	Active-Active
“Minimal cost DR”	Backup & Restore
“Scale automatically”	Auto Scaling Group

If answer does NOT remove single point of failure → eliminate it.

⚡ 4️⃣ Performance Pattern Recognition

When exam says:

Problem	Solution Pattern
High read load RDS	Read Replicas
Microsecond reads	DAX
Frequently accessed static content	CloudFront
Global latency	CloudFront or Multi-Region
High IOPS storage	io2
Session scaling issue	ElastiCache

If option just says “increase instance size” → usually trap.

💰 5️⃣ Cost Optimization Pattern

This is free marks if you memorize it:

Scenario	Best Answer
Predictable 1–3 years	Savings Plan
Interruptible	Spot
Unknown S3 access	Intelligent-Tiering
7-year archive	Glacier Deep Archive
Dev only daytime	Stop/Start
Underutilized EC2	Rightsize

If question says “cost-effective” but option adds extra HA you don’t need → eliminate it.

🎯 6️⃣ The “Least Operational Overhead” Trick

Very common in SAA-C03.

If two answers work, choose the one that is:

✔ Managed service
✔ Serverless
✔ Automatic scaling
✔ Built-in failover

Example:

RDS Multi-AZ > Self-managed EC2 DB
Athena > EC2 running SQL
Auto Scaling > Manual scaling

AWS loves managed services.

🧨 7️⃣ The Trap Pattern (Advanced)

These are designed to confuse you:

Trap 1: Multi-AZ vs Read Replica

Multi-AZ = High Availability

Read Replica = Performance

If question says performance → don’t choose Multi-AZ.

Trap 2: Security Group vs NACL

Need explicit deny? → NACL

Normal allow rules? → Security Group

Trap 3: NAT Gateway vs VPC Endpoint

Private S3 access? → Endpoint

General internet access? → NAT

Trap 4: Backup vs DR

Backup = data recovery

DR = system recovery

If RTO mentioned → it’s DR strategy.

🧮 8️⃣ The “Scope” Rule (Big Scoring Trick)

If problem is:

Instance-level → think Security Group

Subnet-level → think NACL

Account-level → think SCP

Org-wide S3 protection → Block Public Access

Scope matters.

